{
  "$schema": "node_modules/wrangler/config-schema.json",
  "name": "reserva-barber",
  // The generated worker, unwrapped.
  //
  // B7 briefly pointed this at a committed entrypoint that wrapped the
  // generated one in order to add a `scheduled()` handler. It worked and was
  // abandoned on a measurement: anything such an entrypoint imports from `src/`
  // is compiled by wrangler's own esbuild pass, separately from the copy inside
  // `.open-next/server-functions/default/handler.mjs`, so the Prisma query
  // compiler shipped **twice** and the bundle went from 2924 to 3812 KiB gzip —
  // past the free plan's 3 MiB ceiling. The sweep lives in its own Worker
  // instead; see `wrangler.cron.jsonc` and `worker/scheduled.ts`.
  "main": ".open-next/worker.js",
  "compatibility_date": "2025-09-01",
  "compatibility_flags": ["nodejs_compat"],
  "assets": {
    "binding": "ASSETS",
    "directory": ".open-next/assets"
  },
  "observability": {
    "enabled": true
  },
  // APP_ORIGIN is this deployment's own public URL, and it lives HERE rather
  // than in `wrangler secret put` on purpose: it is not a secret — it is the
  // address printed on the link the owner hands to clients — and a value kept
  // only in the Cloudflare dashboard is a value the next deploy from a fresh
  // clone silently lacks.
  //
  // It was optional until B1. `/perfil` falls back to the request's Host header;
  // the public profile page deliberately does NOT (design D3 — a forged Host
  // would otherwise rewrite a shop's OpenGraph tags to point somewhere else).
  // So when this is missing the page still renders, but emits **no canonical URL
  // and no OpenGraph tags at all**, and every link shared to WhatsApp or
  // Instagram arrives as bare text instead of a card. There is no error and no
  // log — the only symptom is a preview nobody sees.
  //
  // Change this in one place when a custom domain replaces the workers.dev one.
  //
  // N1 RAISED THE STAKES ON APP_ORIGIN. It used to degrade only the OpenGraph
  // tags above. It now also composes the link in every confirmation email, so
  // its absence removes the point of the message — and unlike a bad preview, an
  // email cannot be redeployed. The send therefore still goes out (a client who
  // paid is owed the confirmation) with the link omitted entirely, never a
  // relative URL and never a loopback address, and the service logs an error
  // with `reason: "originMissing"`. That log line is the change: this failure is
  // no longer silent.
  //
  // EMAIL_FROM BELONGS HERE AND IS DELIBERATELY ABSENT UNTIL A DOMAIN EXISTS.
  //
  // It is not a secret — it is the address printed on every message the shop
  // sends — so when it exists it goes here, beside APP_ORIGIN, for the same
  // reason: a value kept only in the Cloudflare dashboard is a value the next
  // deploy from a fresh clone silently lacks.
  //
  // It shipped briefly as `Reserva Barber <onboarding@resend.dev>` and was
  // removed, because that value is the worst of the three options. Resend's
  // shared onboarding sender **delivers only to the account owner's own
  // address**: it would have passed a verification done from that inbox and
  // silently dropped every real client — a partial success is far worse here
  // than an absent value, because nothing in the product can detect it.
  //
  // Absent is already a handled, tested state: `createEmailSender` names the
  // missing variable, confirmation emails alone stop, and the booking page's
  // could-not-send line tells the client to keep their link. Set this to a
  // sender on a domain verified in Resend, with SPF/DKIM published, and nothing
  // else changes.
  //
  //   "EMAIL_FROM": "Reserva Barber <turnos@your-verified-domain>"
  "vars": {
    "APP_ORIGIN": "https://reserva-barber.franco-galeano.workers.dev"
  }
  // No `triggers` here. B7's schedule belongs to the sweep Worker, configured
  // in `wrangler.cron.jsonc` — a cron on this Worker would fire a `scheduled`
  // handler that the generated entrypoint does not export.
  // Secrets (DATABASE_URL, PAYMENT_CREDENTIALS_KEY, RESEND_API_KEY) are NOT
  // defined here:
  // - local preview reads .dev.vars (git-ignored)
  // - production uses `wrangler secret put <NAME>`
  //
  // Upload values as EXACT BYTES — pipe from a file or node's stdout, never a
  // shell echo. A BOM or trailing newline is invisible and corrupts the value:
  // for DATABASE_URL that showed up in S0 as an unrelated connection error, and
  // for PAYMENT_CREDENTIALS_KEY it shows up as stored Mercado Pago credentials
  // that cannot be decrypted. For RESEND_API_KEY it shows up as a provider
  // rejection that reads as a wrong key rather than a mangled one — and, since
  // a rejection is non-fatal by design, as clients quietly not being emailed.
  //
  // RESEND_API_KEY belongs on THIS Worker only. The cron Worker sends nothing,
  // and a secret set where it is not needed is a second place to remember when
  // rotating it. It is validated at the confirmation feature's own composition
  // root and never in the global startup check: a global one that threw would
  // take down payment confirmation itself over a missing mail credential.
}
